# A CRLF checkout turns every .sh here into a hard failure: bash reads the trailing carriage
# return as part of the last word, so `set -u` is reached as `set -` and the script exits 2
# before it parses a pattern. Every input answers the same way, which the callers report as
# this check being broken rather than as a verdict on the tag — fail-closed, but on the
# Windows checkout only, which is the one place nothing else here would catch it.
*.sh text eol=lf
